# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kairo environment
# Copy to `.env` inside frontend/ and fill in. Next.js loads it for both the
# client and server runtimes.
#
# `GET /api/health` reports anything missing or degraded, including whether the
# public credentials actually made it into the browser bundle and how many
# independent AI quota buckets you currently hold.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Supabase (required) ---------------------------------------------------
# Project Settings → API. The service-role key bypasses RLS: server-side only,
# never expose it to the browser and never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# IMPORTANT for Docker: the two NEXT_PUBLIC_ values are compiled into the
# browser bundle at BUILD time. Setting them only via `docker run --env-file`
# is too late — there is nothing left to substitute, so the bundle ships
# `undefined` and sign-in silently does nothing while every server route keeps
# working. Pass them as build args (the Dockerfile enforces this).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://your-project.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="your-anon-key"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="your-service-role-key"

# --- Public origin (required behind a proxy) -------------------------------
# The origin a browser uses to reach Kairo, e.g. "https://kairo.example.com".
#
# Behind the reverse proxy on EC2 the app sees a plain-HTTP request on an
# internal host, so it cannot derive its own public URL. Without this, OAuth
# redirects are built from `x-forwarded-*` headers — which works when the proxy
# sets them, and sends learners to http://localhost:3000 when it does not.
#
# It also has to match the callback URL registered under Supabase →
# Authentication → URL Configuration: <APP_ORIGIN>/auth/callback
APP_ORIGIN="http://localhost:3000"

# ===========================================================================
# AI PROVIDERS
#
# Read this section before changing anything in it.
#
# Kairo runs on free upstream tiers, and the one hard fact about free tiers is
# that **quota is metered per API key, per vendor**. No amount of retrying makes
# an exhausted bucket bigger. So the anti-rate-limit strategy is not politeness,
# it is BREADTH: hold several independent buckets and move work between them.
#
# Every provider below is OPTIONAL and at least one is required. A missing key
# removes that provider's models from every fallback chain rather than turning
# them into failed attempts — so a deployment with only GEMINI_API_KEY behaves
# exactly as it always did, just with fewer buckets to draw from.
#
# TWO MULTIPLIERS, both free:
#
#   1. MORE PROVIDERS.  Each vendor is a separate quota.
#   2. MORE KEYS.       *Every* variable below accepts a COMMA-SEPARATED LIST,
#                       and each entry is metered independently by the upstream.
#                       `GROQ_API_KEY="gsk_a,gsk_b"` is genuinely twice the
#                       allowance — the upstream has no idea the two keys belong
#                       to one deployment.
#
# `GET /api/health` reports `ai.buckets` — the total count. For 10–20 daily
# learners aim for 4 or more. Below 3 the health check reports "degraded",
# because one bucket cannot serve that many people whatever the pacing.
#
# WHY THE ORDER CHANGED: an OpenRouter `:free` slug is capped at **50 requests
# per day** on an account holding under 10 lifetime credits. One learner
# building one six-month plan can spend that alone. Groq and Cerebras allow
# roughly 13,000/day each, for free, so the volume tiers now lead on those and
# OpenRouter is kept last in every chain as breadth rather than as a primary.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Groq (strongly recommended — the biggest single win) -------------------
# https://console.groq.com/keys
# ~28 requests/minute and ~13,000/day per model, free, with roughly 200ms to
# first token. It leads the nano and chat chains: the coach went from 3–13s to
# well under a second on this alone.
GROQ_API_KEY="gsk_..."

# --- Cerebras (strongly recommended — separate quota, same class) ----------
# https://cloud.cerebras.ai/platform/apikeys
# Comparable free limits to Groq on entirely separate hardware and quota, which
# is exactly what makes it valuable: a Groq outage does not touch it.
CEREBRAS_API_KEY="csk-..."

# --- Google Gemini (recommended — best structured JSON) --------------------
# https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Free `gemini-2.5-flash` is only ~10 requests/minute and ~250/day, but nothing
# else here matches it for JSON fidelity, so it stays the primary for the
# structured tier (plan blueprints and drill questions) and the high-volume
# tiers are routed away from it.
#
# Two Google accounts = two keys = two 10-RPM allowances:
#   GEMINI_API_KEY="AIza_first,AIza_second"
GEMINI_API_KEY="AIza..."

# --- Mistral (optional) ----------------------------------------------------
# https://console.mistral.ai/api-keys
# The free "Experiment" plan allows ~1 request/second. Needs phone verification.
# MISTRAL_API_KEY="..."

# --- GitHub Models (optional — free with any GitHub account) ----------------
# https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens — scope: models:read
# A fine-grained PAT is enough; there is no billing relationship at all. Modest
# per-minute limit, but it is an entirely separate bucket from everything else
# here, which is the whole reason to add it.
# GITHUB_MODELS_TOKEN="github_pat_..."

# --- Cloudflare Workers AI (optional) --------------------------------------
# https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens — permission: Workers AI (Read)
# Free allowance is 10,000 "neurons"/day rather than a request count; small chat
# calls are cheap in neurons. Needs BOTH values or it stays unconfigured.
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN="..."
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID="..."

# --- Together AI (optional) ------------------------------------------------
# https://api.together.ai/settings/api-keys
# Only the explicitly "-Free" slugs cost nothing.
# TOGETHER_API_KEY="..."

# --- OpenRouter (optional — breadth, not a primary) ------------------------
# https://openrouter.ai/keys
# Kept last in every chain: `:free` slugs allow 20 requests/minute but only 50
# per DAY under 10 lifetime credits (1,000/day above that). Useful as a final
# fallback, unusable as a primary for more than one or two learners.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY="sk-or-v1-..."
SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"
SITE_NAME="Kairo"

# --- Model tiers (optional) ------------------------------------------------
# Routed by job size, not by agent name:
#   NANO       classification + JSON repair   (cheap, fast, high volume)
#   STRUCTURED plan blueprints + drill cards  (needs reliable JSON)
#   CHAT       the coach                      (needs fluency and streaming)
#
# Leave these unset unless you have a reason. The built-in chains are 8–10
# models deep across 5+ vendors, ordered by free-tier throughput, and are
# filtered at call time to whichever providers you actually configured.
#
# Each variable accepts a COMMA-SEPARATED FALLBACK CHAIN, tried in order. A
# single value is promoted to the head of the built-in chain rather than
# replacing it, so overriding the primary never discards its fallbacks.
#
# A slug may carry an explicit `provider:` prefix — `groq:llama-3.3-70b-versatile`
# — which is the only way to say WHICH vendor's copy of a shared model you mean.
# Half of these providers serve the same Llama weights.
#
# Three rules when changing these:
#   1. Verify the slug resolves: `GET /api/health?models=1` probes each tier's
#      primary; `?models=all` probes every model in every chain. Providers retire
#      slugs without notice (`openai/gpt-oss-120b:free` now 404s).
#   2. Do not put two models from the same vendor in the first three slots. Those
#      are the slots a burst actually reaches, so a repeat means one vendor
#      outage costs two attempts instead of one.
#   3. Prefer a model that emits no reasoning tokens, or that lets them be turned
#      off. Reasoning is billed against the same max_tokens budget as the answer,
#      so a reasoning model can spend the whole budget thinking and return
#      nothing. Some free slugs also leak the reasoning trace into `content`
#      (nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free opens with "Here's a thinking
#      process:") — the client detects and rejects that.
# NANO_MODEL="groq:llama-3.1-8b-instant"
# STRUCTURED_MODEL="gemini-2.5-flash"
# CHAT_MODEL="groq:llama-3.3-70b-versatile"

# --- Upstream pacing (optional) --------------------------------------------
# Per-provider overrides for how fast requests may leave and how many run at
# once. The defaults are derived from each vendor's published free-tier RPM in
# `backend/ai/providers.ts`, and the token bucket then RE-LEARNS the real
# ceiling from the 429s and Retry-After headers the upstream actually sends —
# so these rarely need touching. Raise them on a paid key.
#
# AI_MAX_CONCURRENCY_GROQ="6"
# AI_MIN_INTERVAL_GROQ_MS="1000"
# AI_MAX_CONCURRENCY_GEMINI="4"
# AI_MIN_INTERVAL_GEMINI_MS="3300"
# AI_MAX_CONCURRENCY_OPENROUTER="2"

# --- Resource discovery ----------------------------------------------------
# Both optional, but a plan built without them has far weaker material.
# YouTube: quota is 10,000 units/day and search.list costs 100 per call, so a
# plan build uses roughly 1,500–2,500 units. /api/health reports if either is
# missing.
YOUTUBE_API_KEY="your-google-api-key"
TAVILY_API_KEY="tvly-..."

# --- Local development -----------------------------------------------------
# DEMO_MODE bypasses auth entirely and pins every request to DEMO_USER_ID. That
# user row must exist in auth.users (see database/seed-demo-user.sql). Never
# enable this in a deployed environment — and note that with it on, the OAuth
# path is never exercised, so sign-in bugs stay invisible.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_MODE="false"

LOG_LEVEL="info"
